WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.544
Last time, we found our mixture was seventy

00:00:02.585 --> 00:00:05.289
percent salt, by separating it and weighing the

00:00:05.309 --> 00:00:05.670
parts.

00:00:06.311 --> 00:00:08.239
But salt itself is a compound.

00:00:08.681 --> 00:00:10.864
Its sodium and chlorine are bonded in a

00:00:10.985 --> 00:00:14.129
fixed ratio, one sodium for every chlorine, so

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:15.331
no filter can split them.

00:00:16.075 --> 00:00:16.919
Here's the surprise.

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:19.950
Because that ratio is fixed, you don't need

00:00:19.972 --> 00:00:20.432
to split it.

00:00:20.874 --> 00:00:23.404
The formula alone tells you what percentage of

00:00:23.426 --> 00:00:24.971
the mass comes from each element.

00:00:25.635 --> 00:00:27.880
We'll build the method on carbon dioxide, and

00:00:27.921 --> 00:00:29.624
then you'll use it on salt yourself.

00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:31.994
Here's why the formula is enough.

00:00:32.738 --> 00:00:35.063
Every carbon dioxide molecule is the same:

00:00:35.685 --> 00:00:37.930
one carbon atom and two oxygen atoms.

00:00:38.572 --> 00:00:41.056
So whatever fraction of one molecule's mass is

00:00:41.138 --> 00:00:44.183
oxygen, a billion molecules have exactly the same

00:00:44.284 --> 00:00:44.704
fraction.

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:47.152
One molecule is a fair sample of the

00:00:47.233 --> 00:00:47.975
whole substance.

00:00:48.698 --> 00:00:50.976
It's like a carton of identical snack packs.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.229
Find what fraction of one pack is nuts,

00:00:53.672 --> 00:00:54.878
and you know the fraction for the whole

00:00:54.919 --> 00:00:55.260
carton.

00:00:56.029 --> 00:00:57.736
To find those fractions, we need to know

00:00:57.858 --> 00:00:59.866
how heavy each kind of atom is.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.113
Atoms are far too light to weigh one

00:01:02.134 --> 00:01:04.137
at a time, so chemists compare them on

00:01:04.378 --> 00:01:07.003
one scale, called relative atomic mass.

00:01:07.685 --> 00:01:09.673
On that scale, a carbon atom is twelve

00:01:09.714 --> 00:01:12.164
point zero and an oxygen atom is sixteen

00:01:12.225 --> 00:01:12.726
point zero.

00:01:13.209 --> 00:01:15.975
They're just comparison numbers, with no units, and

00:01:15.996 --> 00:01:17.939
you'll find them on the periodic table or

00:01:18.041 --> 00:01:18.621
in the question.

00:01:19.305 --> 00:01:21.036
A later video explains why many of them

00:01:21.218 --> 00:01:22.003
aren't whole numbers.

00:01:22.672 --> 00:01:23.220
Quick prediction.

00:01:23.788 --> 00:01:26.035
Two of the three atoms in carbon dioxide

00:01:26.216 --> 00:01:26.877
are oxygen.

00:01:27.681 --> 00:01:30.512
So is carbon dioxide two thirds oxygen by

00:01:30.574 --> 00:01:30.894
mass,

00:01:31.417 --> 00:01:32.461
about sixty-seven percent?

00:01:33.065 --> 00:01:33.488
Or more,

00:01:34.015 --> 00:01:34.317
or less?

00:01:35.268 --> 00:01:35.631
Have a think.

00:01:39.362 --> 00:01:40.869
It's more, and here's why.

00:01:41.631 --> 00:01:42.673
Add up the whole molecule:

00:01:43.314 --> 00:01:45.658
one carbon at twelve point zero, plus two

00:01:45.739 --> 00:01:47.782
oxygens at sixteen point zero each.

00:01:48.344 --> 00:01:51.481
That's forty-four point zero, the relative formula mass.

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:55.136
The oxygen's share is two times sixteen point

00:01:55.157 --> 00:01:57.084
zero, which is thirty-two point zero.

00:01:57.628 --> 00:02:00.617
So oxygen is thirty-two point zero over forty-four

00:02:00.658 --> 00:02:03.405
point zero, times a hundred, which is seventy-two

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:04.509
point seven percent.

00:02:05.172 --> 00:02:07.916
That's more than two thirds, because each oxygen

00:02:08.058 --> 00:02:10.001
atom is heavier than the carbon atom.

00:02:10.623 --> 00:02:13.051
Counting atoms isn't the same as weighing them.

00:02:13.811 --> 00:02:14.621
So here's the method.

00:02:15.188 --> 00:02:17.115
Count how many atoms of the element are

00:02:17.217 --> 00:02:17.838
in the formula.

00:02:18.402 --> 00:02:21.153
Multiply by its relative atomic mass, to get

00:02:21.194 --> 00:02:22.841
that element's share of the mass.

00:02:23.485 --> 00:02:25.335
Add up every atom in the formula, to

00:02:25.376 --> 00:02:26.683
get the relative formula mass.

00:02:27.429 --> 00:02:29.641
Then divide the share by the total, and

00:02:29.703 --> 00:02:30.929
multiply by a hundred.

00:02:31.492 --> 00:02:33.737
It's part over whole, just like last time,

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:36.383
except now the whole is the formula itself.

00:02:37.089 --> 00:02:38.515
Now a formula with a trap.

00:02:39.078 --> 00:02:41.585
Ammonium nitrate is N H four N O

00:02:41.666 --> 00:02:41.966
three.

00:02:42.588 --> 00:02:45.393
It's made of two parts, ammonium and nitrate,

00:02:45.855 --> 00:02:48.038
and each part contains a nitrogen atom.

00:02:48.562 --> 00:02:50.533
So there are two nitrogens, not one.

00:02:51.339 --> 00:02:53.781
The relative formula mass is fourteen point zero,

00:02:53.962 --> 00:02:56.785
plus four times one point zero, plus fourteen

00:02:56.846 --> 00:02:59.648
point zero, plus three times sixteen point zero,

00:02:59.849 --> 00:03:01.190
which is eighty point zero.

00:03:01.873 --> 00:03:04.464
Nitrogen's share is two times fourteen point zero,

00:03:04.706 --> 00:03:05.649
twenty-eight point zero.

00:03:06.332 --> 00:03:09.136
So nitrogen is twenty-eight point zero over eighty

00:03:09.197 --> 00:03:11.940
point zero, which is thirty-five point zero percent.

00:03:12.642 --> 00:03:15.066
Count only one nitrogen, and you'd get half

00:03:15.107 --> 00:03:17.550
that, seventeen point five percent, which is a

00:03:17.591 --> 00:03:18.211
classic slip.

00:03:18.983 --> 00:03:20.938
Some crystals hold water inside them.

00:03:21.401 --> 00:03:24.347
These blue copper sulfate crystals have water molecules

00:03:24.447 --> 00:03:27.112
built into the crystal itself, called water of

00:03:27.212 --> 00:03:27.973
crystallisation.

00:03:28.655 --> 00:03:30.664
Their formula is written C u S O

00:03:30.745 --> 00:03:32.714
four, dot, five H two O.

00:03:33.397 --> 00:03:35.401
The dot means that for every copper sulfate

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:37.786
unit, there are five water molecules in the

00:03:37.827 --> 00:03:38.127
crystal.

00:03:38.872 --> 00:03:40.806
Treat the five waters as one group.

00:03:41.531 --> 00:03:43.653
Each water is two times one point zero

00:03:43.734 --> 00:03:44.995
plus sixteen point zero,

00:03:45.517 --> 00:03:47.940
eighteen point zero, so the five waters are

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:48.922
ninety point zero.

00:03:49.543 --> 00:03:52.206
The whole formula is sixty-three point five, plus

00:03:52.246 --> 00:03:55.210
thirty-two point one, plus sixty-four point zero, plus

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.233
ninety point zero, which is two hundred and

00:03:57.273 --> 00:03:58.354
forty-nine point six.

00:03:59.035 --> 00:04:01.384
So water is ninety point zero over two

00:04:01.425 --> 00:04:04.114
hundred and forty-nine point six, thirty-six point one

00:04:04.155 --> 00:04:04.496
percent.

00:04:05.269 --> 00:04:07.076
And that number is something you can measure.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.811
Heat the blue crystals gently, and the water

00:04:09.893 --> 00:04:11.119
is driven off as steam.

00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:13.438
The crystals crumble to a white powder.

00:04:14.164 --> 00:04:16.213
If all the water leaves, the mass falls

00:04:16.274 --> 00:04:17.821
by thirty-six point one percent.

00:04:18.465 --> 00:04:20.568
So two point five zero grams of crystals

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:23.193
would lose about zero point nine zero grams,

00:04:23.555 --> 00:04:25.678
leaving about one point six zero grams.

00:04:26.502 --> 00:04:28.269
Last time, lost mass was an error.

00:04:28.652 --> 00:04:29.799
Here, it's the measurement.

00:04:30.456 --> 00:04:31.443
Here's the line to remember.

00:04:32.008 --> 00:04:34.884
Count every atom, then it's part over whole.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:38.015
Count every atom of the element, including the

00:04:38.076 --> 00:04:40.321
ones hiding in a second group or in

00:04:40.342 --> 00:04:43.329
a hydrate's waters, then divide its share by

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:44.432
the whole formula mass.

00:04:47.056 --> 00:04:49.290
Your turn, and it's the salt from the

00:04:49.371 --> 00:04:49.793
start.

00:04:50.518 --> 00:04:54.623
Using sodium, twenty-three point zero, and chlorine, thirty-five

00:04:54.684 --> 00:04:55.343
point five,

00:04:55.905 --> 00:04:58.868
calculate the percentage by mass of chlorine in

00:04:58.969 --> 00:04:59.929
sodium chloride.

00:05:00.491 --> 00:05:01.527
Pause here to answer.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:15.087
The relative formula mass is twenty-three point zero

00:05:15.227 --> 00:05:18.475
plus thirty-five point five, which is fifty-eight point

00:05:18.555 --> 00:05:18.855
five.

00:05:19.517 --> 00:05:22.864
Chlorine is thirty-five point five over fifty-eight point

00:05:22.924 --> 00:05:24.307
five, times a hundred,

00:05:24.808 --> 00:05:26.111
sixty point seven percent.

00:05:27.013 --> 00:05:28.899
One of the two atoms is chlorine, but

00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:30.865
fifty percent is the classic slip.

00:05:31.528 --> 00:05:33.373
A chlorine atom is heavier than a sodium

00:05:33.434 --> 00:05:35.379
atom, so it carries more of the mass.

00:05:37.079 --> 00:05:37.466
Last one.

00:05:38.015 --> 00:05:40.216
Using magnesium twenty-four point three,

00:05:40.758 --> 00:05:42.518
sulfur thirty-two point one,

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:46.722
oxygen sixteen point zero and hydrogen one point

00:05:46.743 --> 00:05:47.002
zero,

00:05:47.564 --> 00:05:50.385
calculate the percentage by mass of water in

00:05:50.466 --> 00:05:52.567
magnesium sulfate heptahydrate,

00:05:53.229 --> 00:05:55.990
M g S O four, dot, seven H

00:05:56.031 --> 00:05:56.351
two O.

00:05:56.996 --> 00:05:57.907
Pause here to answer.

00:06:08.641 --> 00:06:10.788
The magnesium sulfate part adds up to one

00:06:10.848 --> 00:06:12.112
hundred and twenty point four.

00:06:12.794 --> 00:06:15.602
Seven waters at eighteen point zero each make

00:06:15.683 --> 00:06:17.107
one hundred and twenty-six point zero.

00:06:17.788 --> 00:06:20.032
So the total is two hundred and forty-six

00:06:20.092 --> 00:06:20.713
point four,

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:23.218
and water is one hundred and twenty-six point

00:06:23.258 --> 00:06:24.139
zero over that,

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.023
fifty-one point one percent.

00:06:27.066 --> 00:06:29.592
Counting only one water gives thirteen point zero

00:06:29.633 --> 00:06:31.517
percent, and that's the slip to avoid.

00:06:33.549 --> 00:06:36.397
We've been using relative atomic masses as numbers

00:06:36.478 --> 00:06:37.059
handed to us.

00:06:37.481 --> 00:06:39.509
But each one comes from what's inside the

00:06:39.591 --> 00:06:39.811
atom.

00:06:40.554 --> 00:06:42.896
Chemists can write chlorine like this, with a

00:06:42.957 --> 00:06:45.620
thirty-five above and a seventeen below the symbol.

00:06:46.362 --> 00:06:48.283
Next time, we'll see how those two numbers

00:06:48.344 --> 00:06:50.785
tell us exactly which particles are inside an

00:06:50.926 --> 00:06:52.147
atom, or an ion.

00:06:54.861 --> 00:06:55.684
Here are the key notes.

00:06:56.207 --> 00:06:57.755
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:57.795 --> 00:06:58.217
to copy them.
